  "step_2": "The individual reports being attracted by the potentially higher gain in Lottery B (4000€ vs 3000€). They note that the difference in probability (5 percentage points: 25% vs 20%) does not seem large. This suggests the individual considered both the magnitude of the best outcome and the probability of winning, but downplayed the probability difference. The justification centers on the higher potential reward and the perceived smallness of the probability gap. There is no mention of skewness, distribution shape, or the concentration of probability on bad outcomes. The focus is on the trade-off between a higher prize and a slightly lower chance of winning.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, specifically preferring lotteries where more probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes (i.e., more right-skewed). This would favor Lottery B, which has 80% chance of zero (more right-skewed) vs 75% in A. The individual did consider aspects related to the lotteries (payoff size and probability), and ended up favoring B, but their justification was based on the higher potential gain and the small perceived difference in probabilities, not on the concentration of probability on the unfavorable outcome. There is no indication that the individual evaluated the shape of the distribution or thought in terms of skewness. The reasoning is outcome-focused (what I can win) and probability-comparative, but not distributional. Therefore, while the final choice aligns with the decision reason, the underlying justification does not reflect the skewness-based rule. The information considered overlaps partially (probabilities and outcomes), but the rule (prefer more right-skewed) is not mirrored in the verbal justification.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
