  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness—specifically preferring the more right-skewed lottery, which in this case is Lottery B. The individual also appears to favor Lottery B (the higher payoff with lower probability), but for a completely different reason: indifference due to low probabilities and a desire to 'go for the higher win' when it doesn't matter much. The information considered by the individual (low probability of winning, size of prize) overlaps only partially with the information relevant to skewness (probability distribution across favorable vs unfavorable outcomes). However, the individual does not analyze or mention the distributional asymmetry or concentration of probability on bad outcomes as a reason for preference. The justification is outcome-focused ('higher win') rather than structure-focused (distribution shape). Therefore, while the final preference may align with the decision reason (favoring B), the reasoning process is distinct. There is no evidence that skewness was part of the deliberation.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
