  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more right-skewed (i.e., assigns more probability to unfavorable outcomes). As established in Step 1, under a plausible interpretation (favorable = 1000 Euros), Lottery B is more right-skewed because it assigns 100% probability to a non-favorable outcome (500), whereas Lottery A assigns 50% to the favorable outcome. Thus, the reason would prefer B. The individual in the verbal report chooses B (inferred from their preference for certainty), which aligns with the reason’s recommendation. However, the justification is not based on skewness but on the certainty of the outcome. The individual values 'sicher Geld zu bekommen' (getting money for sure), which reflects a standard risk-averse expected utility or certainty effect motivation, not a deliberation about the skewness of the distribution. There is no mention of the concept of unfavorable vs. favorable outcomes in probabilistic terms, nor any comparison of how probability is distributed across outcomes. The information considered (certainty vs. risk) overlaps only partially with the information relevant to skewness (probability distribution over favorable/unfavorable outcomes). The rule used by the individual—prefer certainty—is different from the rule of the decision reason—prefer more right-skewed lottery. While both may lead to the same choice in this case, the underlying rationale differs. Therefore, the match in choice is coincidental, not due to use of the skewness reason. The individual did not use the skewness-based decision rule.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
